After juggling countless SaaS subscriptions, trials, and cancellations, it was time to take a hands-on approach. Reducing reliance on third-party services had become a priority, and my drive for technical experimentation naturally led me to acquire my first dedicated server.

**The objective**

Design a local, sovereign AI infrastructure capable of supporting all my upcoming projects.

## The lab mastery hardware

*The server : Lenovo ThinkCenter M70Q*

{% table %}
- **Composant**
- **Spécification**
- **Implication**
---
- CPU
- Intel Core i5-12400T - \
  6 cœurs / 12 threads, 35 W
- Orchestration and short-lived parallel tasks
---
- iGPU
- Intel UHD 730 (Quick Sync)
- Hardware H.264/HEVC encoding - viable streaming project
---
- RAM
- 2×8 Go DDR4-3200 dual channel
- Upgrade to 32Go (or 64Go) for new projects
{% /table %}

This local setup doesn't allow for conversations with agents or complex tasks. The initial response attempts will be shorted (JSON, label, scores).

\
In this case, I will use small 3–4B models (3 to 4 billion parameters) quantized to Q4 (weights reduced to 4 bits, making them 4× lighter) with an intentionally reduced context window, aiming for an expected throughput of 15–20 tok/s without blowing up memory usage:

- qwen3.5:4b : main
- *gemma4:e4b/phi4-mini* : to be tested
- *bge-m3* : embeddings

That being said, the agents will carry out specifics actions, short iterations and precise tasks.

## OS used

{% table %}
- Layer
- System
- Role
---
- Hypervisor
- Proxmox VE 9.2 (Debian)
- Snapshots, Rollbacks, API Terraform
---
- main VM
- Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS
- All Docker stack
---
- System-conteners
- Debian 13 en LXC
- Tailscale, small services..
{% /table %}

The choice of Proxmox is justified by several key features: it enables instant snapshots before any experimentation, rollbacks in under 30 seconds. And full automation via Terraform thanks to its API.

## The stack

***Foundations***

Docker + Compose · Caddy (TLS via DNS-01) · Tailscale (0 open port) · Gitea + runner · Terraform (provider `bpg/proxmox`) · Ansible

***Data***

PostgreSQL + **pgvector** · Redis · MinIO (local S3, same SDK in cloud) · DuckDB

***Monitoring***

Langfuse (trace, cost, LLM call latency)

***AI***

Ollama (local engine) · **Open WebUI** (chat + voice interface) · BGE-M3 (embeddings) · reranker · **faster-whisper** (transcription) · **Piper** (voice synthesis/TTS) · **n8n** (triggers and plumbing) · **LangGraph** ou **Pydantic AI** (code-first agents)

## Project catalog

### Initial local use cases

#### Document processing & structured extraction

An ingestion pipeline for customer contracts and invoices to automatically extract key information, complete with confidence scoring on the output. It displays source references from the origin document and returns JSON validated against a Pydantic schema. A 4B model is more than capable of handling this workload.

#### GDPR-Compliant anonymization pipeline

To safely process client contracts and invoices, the model detects and replaces personal data (PII) within documents. This anonymization process enables building a *golden dataset* for evaluation and fine-tuning, all while remaining fully RGPD compliant with stored data.

#### Documentation RAG

High precision search across a large document corpus. As I am preparing for the Google Cloud Professional Architect certification, the goal is to ingest all my course materials to enable fast and accurate retrieval. Embedding genetation and reranking are handled locally.

#### Local/API router with savings monitoring

The local model evaluates incoming queries to determine iwhether an expensive API call is warranted, while tracking the money saved in the process.

### Hybrid local/cloud project

#### Autonomous 24/7 Youtube Radio

Building a fully automated Youtube channel. A cloud-based model generates music for six hours each night via API. Once generated, the tracks are fed into a locally looped playlist to maintain a continuous 24/7 Youtube live stream using FFmpeg, RTMP and Intel Quick Sync.
